Yes, absolutely! That's exactly Option 1 — AWS Console. Just follow these steps:

Step 1 — Create VPC
Go to console.aws.amazon.com

Search VPC in the top search bar → click VPC

Click Your VPCs → Create VPC

Fill in:

Name: handson-vpc

IPv4 CIDR: 10.0.0.0/16

Tenancy: Default

Click Create VPC

Step 2 — Create 6 Subnets
Left menu → Subnets → Create subnet

Select VPC: handson-vpc

Create these one by one:

Name	CIDR	Availability Zone
public-subnet-a	10.0.1.0/24	us-east-1a
public-subnet-b	10.0.2.0/24	us-east-1b
private-app-a	10.0.3.0/24	us-east-1a
private-app-b	10.0.4.0/24	us-east-1b
private-db-a	10.0.5.0/24	us-east-1a
private-db-b	10.0.6.0/24	us-east-1b
Step 3 — Create Internet Gateway
Left menu → Internet Gateways → Create internet gateway

Name: handson-igw → Create

Select it → Actions → Attach to VPC → select handson-vpc → Attach

Step 4 — Create NAT Gateway
Left menu → NAT Gateways → Create NAT gateway

Fill in:

Name: handson-nat

Subnet: public-subnet-a

Connectivity: Public

Click Allocate Elastic IP → Create NAT gateway

⏳ Wait ~1 min until status shows Available

Step 5 — Create Route Tables
Public Route Table:

Left menu → Route Tables → Create route table

Name: public-rt, VPC: handson-vpc → Create

Select public-rt → Routes tab → Edit routes → Add route

Destination: 0.0.0.0/0

Target: Internet Gateway → handson-igw

Subnet associations tab → Edit subnet associations → select public-subnet-a and public-subnet-b

Private Route Table:

Create another route table: private-rt, VPC: handson-vpc

Routes → Add route:

Destination: 0.0.0.0/0

Target: NAT Gateway → handson-nat

Subnet associations → select all 4 private subnets

Step 6 — Verify
Left menu → Your VPCs → click handson-vpc → you should see all resources attached

Left menu → Subnets → filter by handson-vpc → should see all 6 subnets

Left menu → Route Tables → check public-rt and private-rt routes

That's it! Go ahead and start from Step 1 — let me know if you get stuck anywhere on any screen.


